{
  "dom_id": "step-q40026",
  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ b+1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$",
  "worked_solution": "Given that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot b+a\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ b-3a-6-8 & =0 \\\\ b-3a & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 5+a\\cdot 6+3\\cdot (b+1)+2\\cdot 5 & =0 \\\\ 5+6a+3b+3+10 & =0 \\\\ 6a+3b & =-18 \\\\ 2a+b & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3a+b=14 \\\\ 2a+b=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-1$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n5a=-20\\,⟹\\,a=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(-4)+b & =14 \\\\ 12+b & =14 \\\\ b & =14-12 \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b=-4+2=-2.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
